WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.250
Four men who had waited for months on the snow-covered plateau of Hardangervidda, living on reindeer moss, were waiting for the basement of a single fertilizer plant.

2
00:00:09.250 --> 00:00:14.660
Because it was the only place in Europe producing the substance Hitler's reactor needed.

3
00:00:14.660 --> 00:00:19.590
And the road to that plant ran along the bottom of an icy gorge two hundred metres deep.

4
00:00:27.790 --> 00:00:36.720
When a factory's name enters history, it is usually not because of what was made there; it is because of the possibility that what was made there planted in other people's minds.

5
00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:46.770
In southern Norway, in the region of Telemark, just above the town of Rjukan, a plant called Vemork stood pressed against the steep wall of a narrow valley as though glued to it.

6
00:00:46.770 --> 00:00:50.100
It was exactly that kind of place.

7
00:00:50.100 --> 00:00:55.830
The work done there was one of the dullest jobs in the world: raw material for fertiliser.

8
00:00:55.830 --> 00:01:02.200
When it opened in 1911, Vemork was the largest hydroelectric power station in the world.

9
00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:06.810
This was the whole of Norway's wealth: water falling from mountains.

10
00:01:06.810 --> 00:01:15.900
The force of the Rjukan waterfall was harnessed to turbines, that force was used to fix the nitrogen out of the air, and from nitrogen came artificial fertiliser.

11
00:01:15.900 --> 00:01:23.390
The company that ran the business, Norsk Hydro, built a hydrogen plant beside the power station in 1929.

12
00:01:23.390 --> 00:01:29.600
Hydrogen was obtained by splitting water with electricity, and that splitting had one strange by-product.

13
00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:32.770
Not all the water in the world is identical.

14
00:01:32.770 --> 00:01:40.900
About 1 hydrogen atom in 6400 carries an extra neutron in its nucleus, and this heavy hydrogen is called deuterium.

15
00:01:40.900 --> 00:01:50.470
When Harold Urey discovered the isotope in 1931, and when a few years later the discovery won him the Nobel Prize, the matter was entirely academic.

16
00:01:50.470 --> 00:01:54.440
Water made with deuterium is called heavy water.

17
00:01:54.440 --> 00:02:02.570
It cannot be told apart from ordinary water by eye, it is slightly denser, it boils at a slightly higher temperature, and its ice sinks.

18
00:02:02.570 --> 00:02:09.900
Its proportion in nature is so low that obtaining it in pure form means putting tonnes of water through the same process again and again.

19
00:02:09.900 --> 00:02:13.070
That was Vemork's secret.

20
00:02:13.070 --> 00:02:21.680
When you split water with electricity continuously in order to make hydrogen, the proportion of heavy water in what is left behind rises by itself.

21
00:02:21.680 --> 00:02:28.690
The plant had something nobody else had: a ready, free, continuously flowing source of heavy water.

22
00:02:28.690 --> 00:02:35.540
In December 1934, Norsk Hydro began the world's first commercial production of heavy water.

23
00:02:35.540 --> 00:02:40.150
For several years its customers were universities and laboratories.

24
00:02:40.150 --> 00:02:44.440
They sold it at 40 dollars a litre, and found few buyers.

25
00:02:44.440 --> 00:02:54.490
At the end of 1939, once it was understood that the uranium nucleus could be split, this plant in a remote valley suddenly became the most strategic factory in the world.

26
00:02:54.490 --> 00:03:01.660
To run a chain reaction in a controlled way, you needed a substance that would slow the neutrons down, a moderator.

27
00:03:01.660 --> 00:03:04.510
Heavy water was among the best there was.

28
00:03:04.510 --> 00:03:11.200
In Germany, the physicist Walther Bothe measured in 1941 that graphite absorbed too many neutrons.

29
00:03:11.200 --> 00:03:18.210
The measurement was wrong, the graphite he used contained impurities, but German work was built on that wrong result.

30
00:03:18.210 --> 00:03:23.780
The Americans went forward with graphite; the Germans condemned themselves to a single road.

31
00:03:23.780 --> 00:03:27.110
That road ran through a fertiliser plant in Telemark.

32
00:03:27.110 --> 00:03:30.440
The Germans were not the first to notice this.

33
00:03:30.440 --> 00:03:44.170
In March 1940, Jacques Allier of French intelligence came to a secret arrangement with Norsk Hydro's management, divided the stock the plant had on hand, 185 kilograms of heavy water, among 26 drums,

34
00:03:44.170 --> 00:03:47.980
and got it out of the country.

35
00:03:47.980 --> 00:03:53.230
The drums went first to France, and when Germany occupied France, on to Britain.

36
00:03:53.230 --> 00:04:00.720
The move was the first sign of how flammable a place Vemork was, and it left the German war machine the plant's empty stores.

37
00:04:00.720 --> 00:04:06.130
On 9 April 1940, Germany invaded Norway.

38
00:04:06.130 --> 00:04:10.420
Like all the country's industry, Vemork changed hands.

39
00:04:10.420 --> 00:04:16.310
Before long the German chemical giant IG Farben ordered Norsk Hydro to increase output.

40
00:04:16.310 --> 00:04:23.320
A plant that before the war could extract around 10 kilograms of heavy water a month was now asked for 100 kilograms.

41
00:04:23.320 --> 00:04:32.730
A high-concentration room was built in the factory basement: a row of 18 stainless steel cells in which the water was brought to its final and most valuable state.

42
00:04:32.730 --> 00:04:39.580
The chemical giant issuing the orders did not know why the Norwegian engineers were writing those orders down so carefully.

43
00:04:39.580 --> 00:04:49.150
Jomar Brun, the manager of the plant's heavy water section, was secretly leaking production figures, factory plans, even photographs of pipes and doors.

44
00:04:49.150 --> 00:04:55.520
The information went to London, to Leif Tronstad, a Norwegian chemist and officer.

45
00:04:55.520 --> 00:05:01.730
Tronstad was the right man for it, because he had worked on the building of the heavy water plant himself.

46
00:05:01.730 --> 00:05:10.340
He had fled the country in 1941 and in London had taken charge of the intelligence and special operations branch of the Norwegian army.

47
00:05:10.340 --> 00:05:16.870
The tables in his hands said one thing: the amount of heavy water going from Vemork to Germany was rising.

48
00:05:16.870 --> 00:05:24.360
In the middle of 1942, Brun too was smuggled out of Norway, and in Britain the two of them sat down around the same table.

49
00:05:24.360 --> 00:05:27.370
There was a single question on that table.

50
00:05:27.370 --> 00:05:38.220
How do you destroy the basement of a seven-storey concrete building carved into a mountainside, standing at the edge of a 200-metre ravine, connected to the world by a single suspension bridge?

51
00:05:38.220 --> 00:05:43.790
Bombing from the air was the first thing that came to mind, and it was eliminated at once.

52
00:05:43.790 --> 00:05:54.640
Seen from an aircraft, the target was a small rectangle at the bottom of a narrow cleft, in the shadow of a rock wall, and the real target, the cells, sat on the building's lowest floor.

53
00:05:54.640 --> 00:06:00.210
With the bombing accuracy of 1942, reaching that basement was a gamble.

54
00:06:00.210 --> 00:06:07.540
There were liquid ammonia tanks beside the plant as well, and a single hit could kill thousands of Norwegians living in the town below.

55
00:06:07.540 --> 00:06:10.870
That left the work to be done on the ground.

56
00:06:10.870 --> 00:06:17.400
The Special Operations Executive in London decided to send a reconnaissance and reception party to Vemork first.

57
00:06:17.400 --> 00:06:30.010
On 18 October 1942, four Norwegians, Jens-Anton Poulsson, Knut Haugland, Claus Helberg and Arne Kjelstrup, were parachuted onto Hardangervidda, the Hardanger plateau.

58
00:06:30.010 --> 00:06:32.860
The operation was called Grouse.

59
00:06:32.860 --> 00:06:38.910
All four had grown up in that country; they knew what Europe's largest mountain plateau did in each season.

60
00:06:38.910 --> 00:06:46.880
Even so, they landed in the wrong place, dragged heavy radio and supply crates across the snow for weeks, and ran out of food.

61
00:06:46.880 --> 00:06:53.250
By the middle of December all four were weak with hunger, boiling moss and lichen on the plateau and drinking it.

62
00:06:53.250 --> 00:06:58.180
On 23 December 1942, Poulsson shot a reindeer.

63
00:06:58.180 --> 00:07:08.550
That animal was what kept the team alive: they ate its meat, its fat, even the half-digested lichen in its stomach, because the vitamin needed to ward off scurvy was there.

64
00:07:08.550 --> 00:07:14.120
While they were waiting for reindeer on the plateau, an entirely different plan had ripened in Britain.

65
00:07:14.120 --> 00:07:24.330
A trained demolition party from the Royal Engineers would be sent to Vemork, the men would be landed on the plateau by glider, and the Grouse team would meet them and take them to the factory.

66
00:07:24.330 --> 00:07:27.020
The operation was called Freshman.

67
00:07:27.020 --> 00:07:35.470
On the evening of 19 November 1942, two Halifax bombers took off from Scotland, each towing a Horsa glider behind it.

68
00:07:35.470 --> 00:07:39.600
Counting the pilots, there were 34 men in the gliders.

69
00:07:39.600 --> 00:07:41.970
Not one of them reached the target.

70
00:07:41.970 --> 00:07:47.540
Off the Norwegian coast the weather broke, the cloud base came down, icing began.

71
00:07:47.540 --> 00:07:53.590
The homing beacon of the Grouse team waiting on the plateau did not give the range that had been hoped for.

72
00:07:53.590 --> 00:08:01.400
One of the aircraft, unable to find its way, flew into a mountain as it turned for home, and its crew of 7 was killed.

73
00:08:01.400 --> 00:08:05.530
The glider it was towing came down as well.

74
00:08:05.530 --> 00:08:12.700
The second glider's tow rope snapped, and the glider smashed into a snow-covered slope hundreds of kilometres from the target.

75
00:08:12.700 --> 00:08:19.710
Some died in the crashes; the survivors, wounded, most of them in uniform, surrendered to German patrols.

76
00:08:19.710 --> 00:08:32.960
A month before this, on 18 October 1942, Hitler had signed the order that went into history as the Commando Order: captured commandos, in uniform or not, were not to be treated as prisoners of war, but killed.

77
00:08:32.960 --> 00:08:36.930
That is what happened to the men who lived through the glider crashes.

78
00:08:36.930 --> 00:08:42.500
One group was shot outside a German barracks near Egersund within hours of being taken.

79
00:08:42.500 --> 00:08:50.790
Another group was interrogated for months, moved to the Grini camp near Oslo, and killed there in January 1943.

80
00:08:50.790 --> 00:08:55.400
Of the 41 men who took part in the operation, not one came back.

81
00:08:55.400 --> 00:08:59.690
This was not only a disaster; it was also a tip-off.

82
00:08:59.690 --> 00:09:05.420
Inside the wrecked gliders were explosive charges, sketch maps and British engineer tools.

83
00:09:05.420 --> 00:09:10.510
The Germans looked at the wreckage and learned exactly what Vemork meant to the Allies.

84
00:09:10.510 --> 00:09:18.960
Over the following weeks the ground around the factory turned into a different place: machine guns and searchlights were set up at both ends of the suspension bridge,

85
00:09:18.960 --> 00:09:27.730
the roads leading to the bridge and the open slopes overlooking the valley were mined, the number of guards was increased, doors were closed with iron shutters.

86
00:09:27.730 --> 00:09:30.900
London had had one attempt, and it had used it.

87
00:09:30.900 --> 00:09:36.950
Getting this news to the four men waiting on the plateau amounted to a few coded sentences by radio.

88
00:09:36.950 --> 00:09:43.800
The team's code name was changed to Swallow, and the order they were given could be summed up in a single word: stay.

89
00:09:43.800 --> 00:09:52.730
In the middle of winter, without supplies, on a plateau swept by German search parties and ski patrols, wait for a second team that might never come.

90
00:09:52.730 --> 00:09:54.780
They waited.

91
00:09:54.780 --> 00:10:01.630
In London, meanwhile, one thing had become clear: the men who went into Vemork would not be British.

92
00:10:01.630 --> 00:10:06.080
They would be Norwegians who knew that valley, that rock, that snow.

93
00:10:06.080 --> 00:10:14.050
On the night of 16 February 1943, six Norwegians jumped from a bomber flying over the Hardanger plateau.

94
00:10:14.050 --> 00:10:17.060
The operation was called Gunnerside.

95
00:10:17.060 --> 00:10:27.430
At its head was Joachim Rønneberg, 23 years old at the time, and with him were Knut Haukelid, Fredrik Kayser, Kasper Idland, Hans Storhaug and Birger Strømsheim.

96
00:10:27.430 --> 00:10:34.120
All were Norwegians, all had trained in sabotage in Britain, and Rønneberg had chosen his team himself.

97
00:10:34.120 --> 00:10:40.810
They came down tens of kilometres from the intended point, and on the night they landed a storm swallowed the plateau.

98
00:10:40.810 --> 00:10:46.700
For five days they sat in a hunting cabin, waiting for the howling to die down.

99
00:10:46.700 --> 00:10:53.390
On 23 February, following ski tracks, they met the four men who had been waiting and starving for months.

100
00:10:53.390 --> 00:11:01.840
The first question that came into Rønneberg's mind when he saw these bearded, worn-out, bony men was whether they had the strength left to walk as far as the factory.

101
00:11:01.840 --> 00:11:04.690
Nine men laid the plan in the cabin.

102
00:11:04.690 --> 00:11:09.140
The bridge was out: searchlight, sentry, mines.

103
00:11:09.140 --> 00:11:14.870
Going round above the valley was out: snow, open slope, tracks.

104
00:11:14.870 --> 00:11:18.680
The only route left was the one nobody would think of trying.

105
00:11:18.680 --> 00:11:30.810
Go down into the valley of the Måna, the river that runs beneath the cliff Vemork stands on, cross the half-frozen river, climb the opposite wall, and come up onto the single railway line that led to the factory.

106
00:11:30.810 --> 00:11:37.340
Local sources said that line had been left unguarded because nobody believed it could be reached by climbing from below.

107
00:11:37.340 --> 00:11:44.030
Helberg went down into the valley and tried the route himself, and when he came back he said one thing: it can be done.

108
00:11:44.030 --> 00:11:52.000
On the evening of 27 February, the six saboteurs and the rest of the party crossed the plateau and came to the edge of the valley.

109
00:11:52.000 --> 00:11:59.810
The weather was too mild for that winter; the thermometer stood above zero, the snow had grown heavy, the ice was cracking.

110
00:11:59.810 --> 00:12:08.260
They climbed down the cliff, crossed the river between blocks of ice, climbed some 150 metres up, and came out onto the railway line.

111
00:12:08.260 --> 00:12:10.630
It was past midnight.

112
00:12:10.630 --> 00:12:15.080
They waited for the change of guard and walked quietly along the rails.

113
00:12:15.080 --> 00:12:19.050
Haukelid's bolt cutters cut the chain on the plant's side gate.

114
00:12:19.050 --> 00:12:28.620
Rønneberg had bought those cutters with his own money from a shop in Cambridge, in Britain, because he had decided that the hacksaw he had been issued would be both slow and loud.

115
00:12:28.620 --> 00:12:31.470
In the factory yard they split in two.

116
00:12:31.470 --> 00:12:36.400
Four men stayed outside with their weapons trained on the guard hut.

117
00:12:36.400 --> 00:12:41.330
Rønneberg and Kayser were the ones who would get into the high-concentration room.

118
00:12:41.330 --> 00:12:44.980
The basement door they had expected to use was locked.

119
00:12:44.980 --> 00:12:50.870
There was a detail on the sketches in London: a cable duct that entered the building from outside.

120
00:12:50.870 --> 00:12:59.480
The two men crawled on their elbows through that duct, among pipes and cables, and dropped into the room itself, directly above the 18 steel cells.

121
00:12:59.480 --> 00:13:04.090
There was one person in the room, a Norwegian watchman named Johansen.

122
00:13:04.090 --> 00:13:06.940
He was unarmed and did not resist.

123
00:13:06.940 --> 00:13:12.510
Rønneberg and Kayser wrapped the explosive charges one by one around the bodies of the cells.

124
00:13:12.510 --> 00:13:20.800
A sound at the window froze them both: two men from the outside party, unable to find the cable duct, had broken the glass and jumped in.

125
00:13:20.800 --> 00:13:23.010
The fuses were fitted.

126
00:13:23.010 --> 00:13:32.100
Rønneberg cut the fuses, which had been set for two minutes, down to 30 seconds; he did not want to leave without hearing that the explosion had actually happened.

127
00:13:32.100 --> 00:13:37.190
Just as they were about to go, the watchman said something: his glasses.

128
00:13:37.190 --> 00:13:43.880
They had been left somewhere in the room, and in wartime Norway having a new pair made was close to impossible.

129
00:13:43.880 --> 00:13:51.210
In that room, with the fuses waiting to be lit and the whole climb still ahead of them, the men looked for a pair of glasses.

130
00:13:51.210 --> 00:13:53.100
They found them.

131
00:13:53.100 --> 00:13:57.710
They told Johansen to run up the stairs, and lit the fuses.

132
00:13:57.710 --> 00:14:02.640
Between thick concrete walls, the explosion gave no more than a muffled thud.

133
00:14:02.640 --> 00:14:10.130
The German sentry outside came out of the hut, looked around, decided a mine had gone off by itself, and went back in.

134
00:14:10.130 --> 00:14:15.060
At that moment nine men were going down from the railway line into the valley.

135
00:14:15.060 --> 00:14:21.590
Behind them, left deliberately in the middle of the room, was one thing: a British Thompson submachine gun.

136
00:14:21.590 --> 00:14:29.080
It was a message that this had been the work of British commandos, and that punishing the local population therefore made no sense.

137
00:14:29.080 --> 00:14:31.450
The message held.

138
00:14:31.450 --> 00:14:37.340
There were interrogations and arrests in Rjukan, but no mass execution.

139
00:14:37.340 --> 00:14:46.430
More than 500 kilograms of heavy water in the cells, the purest form of the product, ran across the floor into the drains and mixed with the water of the Måna.

140
00:14:46.430 --> 00:14:51.520
Not a single shot had been fired, and not one person on either side had died.

141
00:14:51.520 --> 00:14:55.490
The Germans pushed thousands of soldiers onto the plateau.

142
00:14:55.490 --> 00:15:01.860
Five men set off on skis towards Sweden and crossed the border after about 320 kilometres.

143
00:15:01.860 --> 00:15:04.870
Haukelid and Kjelstrup stayed on the plateau.

144
00:15:04.870 --> 00:15:16.520
Helberg got away from a ski chase with a German patrol, fell from a rock face in the dark and broke his arm, and while being treated at a mountain hotel fell into a far stranger danger when Josef Terboven,

145
00:15:16.520 --> 00:15:26.090
the head of the German civil administration in Norway, arrived at the same hotel; he saved himself by jumping from the bus onto which he had been loaded for deportation.

146
00:15:26.090 --> 00:15:34.220
The Special Operations Executive, which had planned the operation, counted it the most successful act of sabotage carried out in the entire war.

147
00:15:34.220 --> 00:15:39.790
But destroying a factory and destroying the machinery in a factory's basement are not the same thing.

148
00:15:39.790 --> 00:15:42.960
The cells could be replaced, and they were.

149
00:15:42.960 --> 00:15:52.530
The Germans piled engineers and materials into Vemork; the plant began producing heavy water again in April and came close to its old capacity by midsummer.

150
00:15:52.530 --> 00:15:58.100
The reports reaching London showed the same curve: production was climbing once more.

151
00:15:58.100 --> 00:16:01.430
What the February night had bought was a few months.

152
00:16:01.430 --> 00:16:05.240
This time there was nothing left to do on the ground.

153
00:16:05.240 --> 00:16:13.370
The factory's defences had hardened one degree further after Gunnerside, and a second team could not be expected to come by the same road.

154
00:16:13.370 --> 00:16:16.860
The decision was handed to the American Eighth Air Force.

155
00:16:16.860 --> 00:16:24.830
Norway's exile government in London was asked what that decision would mean, and the Norwegian officials objected because of the risk to the town below.

156
00:16:24.830 --> 00:16:28.800
Their objection was recorded, and the operation was approved.

157
00:16:28.800 --> 00:16:40.290
On the morning of 16 November 1943, in cloudless weather, 143 B-17 Flying Fortress bombers came over Vemork and dropped 711 bombs.

158
00:16:40.290 --> 00:16:43.940
A wave behind them struck the nitrate plant in Rjukan.

159
00:16:43.940 --> 00:16:51.910
The aircraft took off from Britain, flew into the interior of Norway and returned; the crews sat in their cockpits for close to 10 hours.

160
00:16:51.910 --> 00:16:56.040
Only a very small part of the bombs fell inside the target area.

161
00:16:56.040 --> 00:17:07.050
The narrowness of the valley, the way the target sat hard against the rock wall, the natural spread of bombing from altitude: the result confirmed everything that had been said against bombing in 1942.

162
00:17:07.050 --> 00:17:19.020
The upper floors of the electrolysis building and the power station section were heavily damaged, pipelines and the turbine hall were torn apart, but the basement where the heavy water was concentrated stayed standing,

163
00:17:19.020 --> 00:17:22.190
thanks to the metres of concrete above it.

164
00:17:22.190 --> 00:17:28.080
Most of the bombs that hit anything at all came down on the slopes around the factory and on the edge of Rjukan.

165
00:17:28.080 --> 00:17:32.530
Two of those bombs found a newly built shelter near the plant directly.

166
00:17:32.530 --> 00:17:38.420
The shelter had been put up for exactly such a day, to protect the worker families living there.

167
00:17:38.420 --> 00:17:41.750
After the hit there were two craters where it had stood.

168
00:17:41.750 --> 00:17:47.320
None of those inside survived; most of the dead were women, and there were children among them.

169
00:17:47.320 --> 00:17:52.890
The number of Norwegian civilians killed that day at Vemork and in Rjukan reached 21.

170
00:17:52.890 --> 00:17:58.140
Houses in the town were destroyed, and six households were wiped out completely.

171
00:17:58.140 --> 00:18:05.470
Almost all of the dead were people who had done nothing except live near a factory in their own country that was being run by the enemy.

172
00:18:05.470 --> 00:18:12.480
The Norwegian government conveyed its objection to its allies once again, this time in far harsher language.

173
00:18:12.480 --> 00:18:19.810
In military terms the raid was half a success; heavy water production did not stop, and the cells were still in place.

174
00:18:19.810 --> 00:18:28.100
But in strategic terms the raid changed everything, because what convinced the Germans was not the damage but the prospect of its being repeated.

175
00:18:28.100 --> 00:18:34.310
The reckoning in Berlin was this: this plant in Norway is no longer a place that can be protected.

176
00:18:34.310 --> 00:18:44.200
Commandos had got in once, hundreds of bombs had come down once, a third time could happen at any moment, and the whole hope of the German nuclear programme hung on a single valley.

177
00:18:44.200 --> 00:18:48.330
The decision was to wind up the heavy water business at Vemork.

178
00:18:48.330 --> 00:18:59.820
The machinery of the concentration plant would be dismantled, whatever heavy water was on hand, including the half-processed liquid, would be carried to Germany in barrels, and production would be set up again there,

179
00:18:59.820 --> 00:19:03.470
somewhere bombers worked less comfortably in those years.

180
00:19:03.470 --> 00:19:06.480
The dismantling began in December.

181
00:19:06.480 --> 00:19:14.610
The liquids in the basement, at different concentrations, were emptied into steel barrels, and their labels were written out as potash lye.

182
00:19:14.610 --> 00:19:21.300
There was still a radio operator on the plateau, and there were still Norwegian engineers working inside the factory.

183
00:19:21.300 --> 00:19:27.830
In February 1944 a single sentence of information reached London: the cargo is on its way.

184
00:19:27.830 --> 00:19:36.120
It would come down from Vemork by train, cross a lake by ferry, then go down to the coast by train again and on to Germany by ship.

185
00:19:36.120 --> 00:19:40.250
The one fragile point on the route was obvious: the lake.

186
00:19:40.250 --> 00:19:48.220
London's man on the Hardanger plateau was Knut Haukelid, one of the two who had stayed behind instead of going to Sweden after the Gunnerside night.

187
00:19:48.220 --> 00:19:53.470
When the order reached him there was almost no time left: destroy the shipment.

188
00:19:53.470 --> 00:20:00.800
What stood in front of Haukelid was not a military calculation but a moral one, and he later described it plainly himself.

189
00:20:00.800 --> 00:20:09.730
Blowing the cargo up on the train was all but impossible along a route where German patrols moved up and down the rails, and doing it at the coast would be too late.

190
00:20:09.730 --> 00:20:18.020
The only place was Tinnsjø, Lake Tinn, and the vessel that crossed that lake was a passenger ferry the people of the district used every day.

191
00:20:18.020 --> 00:20:25.350
The railway ferry Hydro carried the wagons on her deck, and on the same crossing there travelled villagers, officials and children.

192
00:20:25.350 --> 00:20:28.360
Sinking the ferry meant killing Norwegians.

193
00:20:28.360 --> 00:20:37.930
Haukelid tried to keep the harm to a minimum together with Alf Larsen, an engineer working at Vemork, the local resistance man Rolf Sørlie, and Knut Lier-Hansen.

194
00:20:37.930 --> 00:20:43.820
Sunday was chosen, because the Sunday crossings carried the fewest passengers.

195
00:20:43.820 --> 00:20:54.350
The place of the explosion was calculated as well: the ship had to be struck at the deepest part of the lake, beyond swimming distance from the shore, but at a point where she would go down quickly.

196
00:20:54.350 --> 00:21:01.520
About 8.4 kilograms of plastic explosive were placed below the waterline, against the inner hull at the bow.

197
00:21:01.520 --> 00:21:10.770
The aim was not to tear the hull open but simply to make the bow take water: with the ship going down by the head, her propeller and rudder would come out of the water,

198
00:21:10.770 --> 00:21:15.220
and the captain would be able neither to steer nor to run her aground.

199
00:21:15.220 --> 00:21:19.350
The firing mechanism was made from two alarm clocks.

200
00:21:19.350 --> 00:21:26.360
On the night of 19 to 20 February 1944, Haukelid and Lier-Hansen went aboard.

201
00:21:26.360 --> 00:21:31.450
There was neither a sentry nor a lock on deck; most of the crew were playing cards.

202
00:21:31.450 --> 00:21:38.620
A Norwegian watchman they met on the lower deck was told they were running from the Gestapo, and the man personally showed them where to hide.

203
00:21:38.620 --> 00:21:45.470
The explosive went into the bilge, the alarm clocks were set, and the two left the ship and parted in the dark.

204
00:21:45.470 --> 00:21:54.240
On the morning of Sunday 20 February 1944, the Hydro set out with the wagons carrying the barrels of heavy water on her deck.

205
00:21:54.240 --> 00:21:59.490
At 10.45, in the middle of the lake, there was an explosion forward.

206
00:21:59.490 --> 00:22:06.500
Within a few minutes the ship went down by the bow, and the wagons broke loose from their fastenings and slid into the water.

207
00:22:06.500 --> 00:22:10.310
29 of the crew and passengers were saved.

208
00:22:10.310 --> 00:22:16.680
18 people died, 14 of them Norwegian crew and passengers, 4 of them German soldiers.

209
00:22:16.680 --> 00:22:20.970
The ship went down to a depth of 430 metres and stayed there.

210
00:22:20.970 --> 00:22:23.820
The barrels went down there too.

211
00:22:23.820 --> 00:22:30.990
A few of them stayed on the surface for a while; the liquid inside was a little heavier than water, but the barrel itself floated.

212
00:22:30.990 --> 00:22:36.560
The Germans reported recovering some of them, but the shipment as a whole never reached Germany.

213
00:22:36.560 --> 00:22:42.930
The fluid the German nuclear programme had worked so hard for settled on the bottom of one of Norway's deepest lakes.

214
00:22:42.930 --> 00:22:47.540
After this, heavy water production was never established in Germany.

215
00:22:47.540 --> 00:23:01.750
In March 1945, in the town of Haigerloch in southern Germany, in a rock cellar beneath a church, German physicists ran their last experimental reactor: 664 uranium cubes strung on chains,

216
00:23:01.750 --> 00:23:05.400
with about 1.5 tonnes of heavy water around them.

217
00:23:05.400 --> 00:23:10.650
The arrangement produced neutrons, but it never reached a self-sustaining reaction.

218
00:23:10.650 --> 00:23:18.780
The calculations showed that for the same design to work, roughly one and a half times the uranium they had and far more heavy water would be needed.

219
00:23:18.780 --> 00:23:25.790
In April an American intelligence team called Alsos entered the cellar and collected the cubes and the notebooks.

220
00:23:25.790 --> 00:23:30.400
Only then was it fully understood how far Germany had been from building a bomb.

221
00:23:30.400 --> 00:23:35.970
That opened the meaning of those nights in Telemark to argument, and the argument is still going on.

222
00:23:35.970 --> 00:23:45.220
Postwar documents showed that the German programme had been behind from the beginning in resources, staff and political backing, and had stayed years away from a bomb.

223
00:23:45.220 --> 00:23:51.430
Even if the heavy water had never been cut off, the cellar at Haigerloch was not going to produce one.

224
00:23:51.430 --> 00:24:00.040
But nobody knew that in 1942; the only information on London's table was that the side running the sole source of heavy water was Germany.

225
00:24:00.040 --> 00:24:04.650
The decision was made with the knowledge that existed.

226
00:24:04.650 --> 00:24:10.700
Most of the people who built this story did not see its end, or saw it with difficulty.

227
00:24:10.700 --> 00:24:19.630
Leif Tronstad, who was behind all the plans, returned to Norway in the autumn of 1944 and, while leading the resistance in Telemark,

228
00:24:19.630 --> 00:24:26.480
was shot and killed in March 1945 during a raid in which he had taken a pro-German local official into custody.

229
00:24:26.480 --> 00:24:34.130
Vemork's heavy water building was demolished in 1977, with the traces of its concrete foundations left on the site.

230
00:24:34.130 --> 00:24:44.180
The old power station is today the Norwegian Industrial Workers Museum, and the same halls hold exhibitions both of these acts of sabotage and of the labour history of a mountain town.

231
00:24:44.180 --> 00:24:50.550
This industrial heritage at Rjukan and Notodden was placed on the World Heritage list in 2015.

232
00:24:50.550 --> 00:24:58.680
In 2004 a diving team went down to the bottom of the lake and brought one of the barrels, number 26, up to the surface.

233
00:24:58.680 --> 00:25:05.210
The heavy water concentration of the liquid inside matched the low figure written on the German shipping list,

234
00:25:05.210 --> 00:25:12.220
and it became clear that the greater part of the cargo had been half-processed water being carried to Germany to be concentrated again.

235
00:25:12.220 --> 00:25:18.430
What lies at the bottom of the lake, in other words, is not the heart of a bomb but half of the road towards one.

236
00:25:18.430 --> 00:25:26.080
Joachim Rønneberg, who commanded Gunnerside, lived a long life after the war and died in 2018 at the age of 99.

237
00:25:26.080 --> 00:25:32.610
He never liked being called a hero, and whenever he spoke about that night he said the same two things.

238
00:25:32.610 --> 00:25:39.620
The first was that the secret of success had not been courage but weeks of preparation and a team whose members knew one another.

239
00:25:39.620 --> 00:25:46.630
The second was that the only reason what they had lived through was worth telling at all was to show young people how meaningless war is.

240
00:25:46.630 --> 00:25:56.840
Inside the same story there are also the engineers shot near Egersund, the women and children who died in the shelter at Vemork, and the Norwegian passengers who drowned in Lake Tinn.

241
00:25:56.840 --> 00:26:07.370
The story of the skiers who sank Hitler's heavy water is the story of a series of decisions taken in one valley, and most of those who paid for those decisions were never in the room where they were made.

242
00:26:07.370 --> 00:26:10.860
Nöron History'de bugünkü bölümün sonuna geldik.

243
00:26:10.860 --> 00:26:14.510
Yeni bir hikâyede yeniden buluşmak üzere.
